    correctIndex: 2,
    explanationQuick:
'The argument praises the report because silence would have left thousands of patients exposed to a drug backed by fake data. The justifying principle must connect reporting to preventing harm to others — choice (C). The distractors make reporting merely optional, shift to drug-company duties, punish the colleague instead of justifying the report, or cite an irrelevant statistic.',
    explanationWalkthrough:
'Find the conclusion and its support. Conclusion: reporting the fraud was the right thing. Premise: the fake data supported a drug prescribed to thousands. The reasoning links the rightness of reporting to the danger silence would have permitted — patients harmed by a drug approved on false evidence. A justifying principle must therefore make the duty to report turn on risk to others. Choice (C) does exactly that: professionals should report misconduct when silence would endanger others. Applied here, silence would have left thousands at risk, so reporting was right. Choice (A) is the too-weak trap: "may report if comfortable" makes reporting optional and cannot justify the judgment that she did the right thing — it would equally permit staying silent. Choice (B) shifts scope from a researcher\'s duty to report to a company\'s duty to publish, a different subject. Choice (D) draws a conclusion about punishing the colleague, while the argument\'s conclusion is about the rightness of Alvarez\'s report — wrong conclusion. Choice (E) offers a statistical claim about how fraud is discovered; even if true, it supplies no rule connecting reporting to rightness, so it cannot justify the inference.',
    choiceExplanations: [
'This makes reporting merely permissible and dependent on comfort, which cannot justify the claim that Alvarez did the right thing — it would equally bless her staying silent. A principle too weak to license the conclusion is the too-weak trap.',
'This imposes a duty on drug companies to publish data, but the argument is about a researcher\'s decision to report a colleague. Because it changes the subject from the report to corporate disclosure, it is the scope-shift trap.',
'Correct. The argument\'s defense of the report rests on the danger silence posed to patients. This principle makes risk to others the trigger for a duty to report, and applying it here yields exactly the conclusion that reporting was right.',
'The argument concludes that the report was the right thing to do; it never addresses what punishment the colleague deserves. A principle licensing punishment draws a different conclusion and is the wrong-conclusion trap.',
'How fraud is usually discovered is irrelevant to whether this report was right. This statistical claim provides no rule linking the premises to the conclusion, so it is the unsupported trap — true or not, it justifies nothing here.',
    ],
    generalLesson:
'For "did the right thing" conclusions, the principle must supply a duty or permission strong enough to produce that judgment — mere possibility ("may if comfortable") cannot carry a verdict of rightness.',
