'The argument is compact: premise one, the quote was misattributed (a factual error); premise two, the harmed person demanded a correction; conclusion, the paper should print one. The bridging principle must link "factual error plus request from the harmed person" to "should correct" — choice (D). Each distractor fails a basic test. Choice (A) justifies the opposite of the conclusion. Choice (B) restates the misattribution premise; a repeated fact is not a rule and cannot justify the "should" in the conclusion. Choice (C) is too strong: it demands full retraction for any error however trivial, while the argument asks only for a correction of this harmful error — the argument never claims every typo requires a retraction. Choice (E) is the tempting irrelevant-true: verifying quotes before publishing is good practice, but it is forward-looking advice that does nothing to justify printing a correction after the error has occurred.',
