'Principle: A manufacturer is liable for a product defect only if the defect was present when the product was sold.',
    stem: 'Which one of the following most clearly conforms to the principle stated above?',
    choices: [
      { text: 'A toaster overheats because its owner used it to dry wet mittens; the manufacturer is held liable for the resulting fire' },
      { text: 'A chair collapses under a 400-pound load though it was rated for 250 pounds; the maker is held liable on the grounds that the failure proves a defect existed at sale' },
      { text: 'A phone battery explodes, but no evidence shows when the defect arose; the manufacturer is held liable anyway' },
      { text: "A ladder's welds were cracked during manufacturing, but the injured buyer is denied recovery because she bought the ladder on sale" },
      { text: "A crib's slats were glued improperly at the factory and later snap, injuring an infant; the manufacturer is held liable" },
    ],
    correctIndex: 4,
    explanationQuick:
'The principle makes defect-at-time-of-sale a necessary condition for liability. Choice (E) is the clean fit: the defect (bad gluing) existed at manufacture/sale, and liability follows. The other choices impose liability without the required condition, deny it on irrelevant grounds, or treat overloading as proof of a pre-sale defect.',
    explanationWalkthrough:
'The principle has the form "liable only if defect present at sale" — defect-at-sale is necessary for liability. So a conforming case must show the defect predated the sale before imposing liability, and must not impose liability where that condition is unmet or deny it for unrelated reasons. Choice (E) fits: the slats were glued improperly at the factory (defect present at sale), the crib later fails, and the manufacturer is held liable — the necessary condition is satisfied and the result follows. Choice (A) imposes liability where the failure came from misuse after sale, violating the only-if condition — a misapplication. Choice (B) is subtler: the chair failed under more than its rated load, which does not show a defect existed at sale; treating the failure itself as proof is the half-right trap, since the necessary condition is assumed rather than shown. Choice (C) imposes liability with no evidence about when the defect arose — the necessary condition is simply missing, so liability cannot conform to the principle. Choice (D) gets the defect-at-sale condition right but denies recovery because the ladder was bought on sale, a factor the principle never mentions — the wrong conclusion on an irrelevant ground.',
    choiceExplanations: [
'The toaster failed because of misuse after the sale, so the defect was not present when sold. Imposing liability anyway violates the principle\'s only-if condition; this is the wrong-conclusion trap.',
'This is tempting because it mentions a defect at sale, but a chair breaking under nearly double its rated load does not show the defect predated the sale — the required condition is assumed, not established. This is the half-right trap.',
'Here liability is imposed with no evidence at all about when the defect arose. Since defect-at-sale is a necessary condition, liability without it cannot conform to the principle; this is the unsupported trap.',
'The defect was present at manufacture, so the principle\'s condition is met — but recovery is denied because the ladder was bought on sale, a ground the principle never mentions. Denying the result on irrelevant grounds is the wrong-conclusion trap.',
'Correct. The improper gluing existed when the crib was made and sold, satisfying the principle\'s necessary condition, and the manufacturer is held liable — a direct application of the rule.',
    ],
    generalLesson:
'"Only if" makes a condition necessary: before applying the result, verify the condition is actually established — not assumed, not missing, and not replaced by an irrelevant factor.',
